class:: SpecEntropy
summary:: Shannon entropy of spectral energy
categories:: UGens>Analysis>MIR
related:: Guides/MIR2, Classes/SpecCentroid2

description::
Let p = w / sum(w). Returns -sum(p * log(p)). The default divides this by log(number of bins), giving 0 for a single occupied bin and 1 for a flat spectrum. Zero terms contribute zero.

For these formulas w is the raw FFT magnitude or its square, and f is the bin-center frequency in Hz. Silence returns 0. The reader preserves the FFT chain. Shannon entropy applied to the normalized spectrum, using the energy convention described by Peeters (2004).

CPU cost is linear in the number of FFT bins; no memory is allocated while processing. It adds no lookahead beyond the upstream FFT. At a 1024-sample window and 48 kHz the initial analysis window is about 21.3 ms. Changing FFT size or window affects unnormalized values.

Like vanilla link::Classes/SpecCentroid::, this reads FFT frames and produces control-rate timbre information; this class measures shannon entropy of spectral energy instead of the average frequency.

classmethods::
method:: kr

argument:: chain
FFT chain from an FFT with the same literal fftSize. New-frame values update the output; between frames the previous output is retained.

argument:: mode
Literal symbol: \normalized gives entropy in 0..1, \natural gives nats, and \bits uses log2.

argument:: minFreq
Lowest included bin center in Hz; default 0 includes DC.

argument:: maxFreq
Highest included bin center in Hz; 0 selects Nyquist. Empty ranges return 0.

argument:: spectrumMode
Literal 0 uses magnitude; literal 1 uses squared magnitude (power).

argument:: fftSize
Literal power of two from 256 through 8192. Must match the FFT buffer.

returns::
One control-rate descriptor value.

examples::
SpecEntropy computes the Shannon entropy of the power spectrum (0 for a pure sinusoidal tone, approaching 1 for white noise). In this example, high spectral entropy routes the audio into a diffuse multi-tap delay network, while low entropy preserves direct focused sound. An interactive code::MIR2GUI.scope:: displays real-time normalized spectral entropy.
code::
(
s.waitForBoot({
    var path = MIR2GUI.soundPath("texture.wav");
    ~mir2Buffer = if(path.notNil) { Buffer.read(s, path) } { nil };
    ~mir2Bus = Bus.control(s, 1);
    s.sync;
    ~mir2Example = {
        var source = if(~mir2Buffer.notNil) {
            PlayBuf.ar(1, ~mir2Buffer, BufRateScale.kr(~mir2Buffer), loop: 1)
        } {
            // Synthetic texture fallback
            PinkNoise.ar * LFNoise1.kr(1).range(0.2, 0.8) + SinOsc.ar(LFNoise0.kr(2).range(200, 800), 0, 0.2)
        };
        // For live input instead: source = SoundIn.ar(0);

        var chain = FFT(LocalBuf(1024), source, 0.25);
        var entropy = SpecEntropy.kr(chain, mode: \normalized, minFreq: 40, maxFreq: 12000, fftSize: 1024);
        var entLag = entropy.lag(0.08);
        var scatter = CombC.ar(source, 0.1, SinOsc.kr([0.3, 0.4]).range(0.02, 0.07), 1.2) * entLag * 0.4;
        var direct = source * (1 - (entLag * 0.5));
        var sound = (direct ! 2) + scatter;

        if(~mir2Bus.notNil) { Out.kr(~mir2Bus, entropy.clip(0, 1)) };
        sound * Line.kr(0, 1, 0.2) * 0.3
    }.play;

    ~mir2GUI = MIR2GUI.scope("Spectral Entropy", ~mir2Bus, 0.0, 1.0, "norm");
});
)
::
code::
(
~mir2Example.free;
if(~mir2GUI.notNil) { ~mir2GUI.close };
if(~mir2Bus.notNil) { ~mir2Bus.free };
if(~mir2Buffer.notNil) { ~mir2Buffer.free };
)
::

